### What is the Power Set problem?

Given a set of n distinct elements, generate all possible subsets, also called the power set. A set of n elements has exactly 2^n subsets, including the empty set and the full set.

This is LeetCode 78.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Input: [1, 2, 3]
// Output: [], [1], [2], [3], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3], [1,2,3]
// Count: 2^3 = 8 subsets
```

### How does the backtracking approach work?

Use a start index and a running path list. At every recursive call, first record the current path as a valid subset. Then loop from the start index, and for each element add it to the path, recurse with the next index, and remove it to backtrack.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Subsets {

    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start,
                           List<Integer> path,
                           List<List<Integer>> result) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); // record a COPY

        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            path.add(nums[i]);           // include
            backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, result); // recurse
            path.remove(path.size() - 1); // exclude (backtrack)
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Subsets solver = new Subsets();
        System.out.println(solver.subsets(new int[]{1, 2, 3}));
    }
}
```

```text
[[], [1], [1, 2], [1, 2, 3], [1, 3], [2], [2, 3], [3]]
```

### Why record at every call, not just at leaves?

Every prefix of choices is itself a complete subset. The empty path at the very first call is the empty subset. After adding element 1, the path `[1]` is the subset {1}. After adding 1 and 2, `[1,2]` is {1,2}. Recording at every call captures all subsets, while recording only at leaves would miss all subsets shorter than n.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RecordingExplanation {
    // Recursion tree for [1, 2, 3]:
    // Call 1: path=[]         -> record []
    //   Call 2: path=[1]      -> record [1]
    //     Call 3: path=[1,2]  -> record [1,2]
    //       Call 4: path=[1,2,3] -> record [1,2,3] (leaf)
    //     Call 5: path=[1,3]  -> record [1,3] (leaf)
    //   Call 6: path=[2]      -> record [2]
    //     Call 7: path=[2,3]  -> record [2,3] (leaf)
    //   Call 8: path=[3]      -> record [3] (leaf)
}
```

### Why must you store a copy?

The path list is mutated throughout the search. If you stored the reference directly with `result.add(path)`, every recorded entry would point to the same object and reflect its final empty state. Always use `result.add(new ArrayList<>(path))`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CopyBugDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();

        path.add(1);
        result.add(path);              // WRONG: stores reference
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); // CORRECT: stores copy
        path.add(2);

        System.out.println("Reference: " + result.get(0)); // [1, 2] - mutated!
        System.out.println("Copy:      " + result.get(1)); // [1] - safe
    }
}
```

```text
Reference: [1, 2]
Copy:      [1]
```

### What are the alternative approaches?

Three methods produce the same power set:

| Approach | Description | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Backtracking | Recursive add-recurse-remove | Constrained variants (size limits, duplicates) |
| Iterative cascade | For each element, copy all existing subsets and add the element | Simple iterative code |
| Bitmask | Each integer 0 to 2^n-1 is a subset; bit i = include element i | Very compact |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetsBitmask {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int total = 1 << n; // 2^n
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int mask = 0; mask < total; mask++) {
            List<Integer> subset = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) {
                    subset.add(nums[i]);
                }
            }
            result.add(subset);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SubsetsBitmask solver = new SubsetsBitmask();
        System.out.println(solver.subsets(new int[]{1, 2, 3}));
    }
}
```

```text
[[], [1], [2], [1, 2], [3], [1, 3], [2, 3], [1, 2, 3]]
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty input**: one subset, the empty set.
- **Single element**: two subsets, `[]` and `[element]`.
- **Large n**: 2^n grows fast; n = 20 gives about a million subsets.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SubsetEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Subsets solver = new Subsets();
        System.out.println("Empty: " + solver.subsets(new int[]{}));
        System.out.println("Single: " + solver.subsets(new int[]{5}));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: [[]]
Single: [[], [5]]
```

### How do you handle duplicates (Subsets II)?

If the input contains duplicates (LeetCode 90), sort first and skip a candidate when it equals the previous one at the same recursion level. This prevents generating identical subsets.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetsWithDuplicates {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start,
                           List<Integer> path,
                           List<List<Integer>> result) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue; // skip dup
            path.add(nums[i]);
            backtrack(nums, i + 1, path, result);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SubsetsWithDuplicates solver = new SubsetsWithDuplicates();
        System.out.println(solver.subsetsWithDup(new int[]{1, 2, 2}));
    }
}
```

```text
[[], [1], [1, 2], [1, 2, 2], [2], [2, 2]]
```

### What about the banking domain use case?

At the National Bank of India, the marketing team needs every possible combination of optional account add-on services to create pricing packages. The backtracking generates all 2^n combinations, from the empty package to the full bundle.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class ServicePackageGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] services = {"OverdraftProtection", "ChequeBook", "OnlineBanking"};
        int[] indices = {0, 1, 2};
        Subsets solver = new Subsets();
        List<List<Integer>> packages = solver.subsets(indices);
        System.out.println("Service packages (" + packages.size() + " total):");
        for (List<Integer> pkg : packages) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("  {");
            for (int i = 0; i < pkg.size(); i++) {
                if (i > 0) sb.append(", ");
                sb.append(services[pkg.get(i)]);
            }
            sb.append("}");
            System.out.println(sb);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Service packages (8 total):
  {}
  {OverdraftProtection}
  {OverdraftProtection, ChequeBook}
  {OverdraftProtection, ChequeBook, OnlineBanking}
  {OverdraftProtection, OnlineBanking}
  {ChequeBook}
  {ChequeBook, OnlineBanking}
  {OnlineBanking}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How many subsets are there? (2^n for n distinct elements.)
- What if there are duplicates? (Sort and skip consecutive duplicates at the same level: Subsets II.)
- Can you do it without recursion? (Yes, bitmask or iterative cascade.)
- Why do you record at every call? (Every prefix is a valid subset, not just full-length ones.)
- Why do you copy the path? (The path mutates; storing the reference would corrupt all recorded subsets.)
